=== Category B: shared-helper -match/-notmatch sites (2 findings) ===

Two functions T-001 authored (tests/design-system-contract.tests.ps1:249-266,
:284-289) are shared across multiple Test IDs and read every line via a
single, unconditional plain `-match` (case-INsensitive by default, PS7.6.2
confirmed). Their .sh counterparts are NOT uniformly case-insensitive, which
this task's sweep is required to catch mechanically rather than trust the
file's own header comment (:168-177) at face value.

--- F-1: Get-SectionBetween (:249-266) vs section_between() (.sh :179-185) ---

Get-SectionBetween's start/end matching (:254 `-match`, :260 `-match` +
`-notmatch`) is unconditionally case-INSENSITIVE. It backs $loopLines,
$boundariesLines, $capLines, $bsiUiBulletLines, $wfgSectionLines -- the
section-scoped text nearly every TEST-NNN assertion in Category A above
reads from.

The .sh twin, section_between() (:179-185), is implemented with awk's `~`
operator (`$0 ~ start`, `$0 !~ start`), which is case-SENSITIVE by default
in POSIX/gawk with no `-i`/IGNORECASE set anywhere in this suite --
uniformly, for all 5 of its call-sites (LOOP_SECTION, BOUNDARIES_SECTION,
DSL_CAP_SECTION, BSI_UI_BULLET, WFG_SECTION).

DIVERGENCE: the .ps1 side is unconditionally more permissive (case-
insensitive) than the .sh side (case-sensitive), for the SAME section-
boundary-finding operation, across all 5 uses -- not situational, systemic.

Operator-level mis-cased negative fixture (verbatim function bodies copied
from the real suite, run against a heading mis-cased as "## loop" instead of
"## Loop"):

  $ sh sweep-operator.sh
  === section_between() (.sh, awk ~ operator) case-sensitivity ===
  --- mis-cased heading '## loop' against pattern '^## Loop$' (must be EMPTY) ---
  result=[]
  EMPTY (correct: case-sensitive, no match)
  --- correctly-cased heading '## Loop' against pattern '^## Loop$' (must be NON-EMPTY) ---
  result=[## Loop
  consent already holds for this feature and this session
  this line SHOULD be extracted, case matches exactly]
  NON-EMPTY (correct: match found)

  $ pwsh -NoProfile -File sweep-operator.ps1
  === Get-SectionBetween() (.ps1, plain -match operator) case-sensitivity ===
  --- mis-cased heading '## loop' against pattern '^## Loop$' (must be EMPTY if parity with .sh held) ---
  count=3
  NON-EMPTY -- DIVERGENCE: .ps1 -match is case-INSENSITIVE, matched '## loop' where .sh's awk ~ (case-sensitive) would not
    > ## loop
    > consent already holds for this feature and this session
    > this line should not be extracted if section matching is case-sensitive
  --- correctly-cased heading '## Loop' against pattern '^## Loop$' (must be NON-EMPTY) ---
  count=3
  NON-EMPTY (correct: match found)

Confirmed: the .sh side rejects the mis-cased fixture (empty result, no
section found); the .ps1 side accepts it (non-empty, 3 lines extracted) --
a real, mechanically demonstrated divergence.

No cmdlet-layer analogue for this finding: Get-SectionBetween is
implemented with the -match operator only, no Select-String / -split /
[regex] static method / switch -wildcard / Sort-Object involved (confirmed
by the exhaustive enumeration above: 0 such cmdlet/language-feature sites
anywhere in this section).

--- F-2: Get-FirstLineIndex (:284-289), TEST-037 call only ---

Get-FirstLineIndex is a single-definition helper (one `-match` at :286),
called 7 times: 4x by TEST-010, 2x by TEST-025, 1x by TEST-037
(Test-037Unchanged, :601, the CHANGELOG.md anchor search).

For TEST-010 and TEST-025, the .sh counterpart is loop_line_of() (:235-237),
which uses `grep -n -iE` -- case-INsensitive, matching Get-FirstLineIndex's
default. Correct pairing for 6 of the 7 calls.

For TEST-037, the .sh counterpart is NOT loop_line_of() -- it is a direct,
inline `grep -n 'design-sync-loop`' "$CHG"` (.sh :530, inside
test_037_unchanged()), with no `-i` flag: case-SENSITIVE. Get-FirstLineIndex
being unconditionally case-insensitive means this single call-site (of 7)
is paired with a case-sensitive .sh check while using a case-insensitive
.ps1 mechanism.

DIVERGENCE: situational (this one call-site only; the other 6 calls through
the same helper are correctly paired).

